# Aequitas — TLS reverse proxy for a validator node (Contabo).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# Until 2026-08-14 the public HTTPS entry point of this network was hosted on
# Railway, which terminated TLS itself. Railway is decommissioned; the network
# now runs on the two Contabo boxes, which serve the node directly on :8080
# over plain HTTP. Two things in the codebase require real TLS on a real
# hostname, so this is not optional:
#
#   1. isAllowedPeerURL (x/humanity/keeper/sync_blocks.go) accepts http:// ONLY
#      for literal public IPs. For a HOSTNAME it requires https:// — that check
#      exists to stop DNS rebinding. So "https://aequitas.digital" can only
#      ever work as a peer seed if the hostname genuinely serves TLS.
#   2. The browser-facing pages (explorer, /dapp) use getUserMedia (camera) and
#      wallet APIs, which browsers gate on a secure context.
#
# STATUS AS FOUND (2026-08-14, verified live)
# -------------------------------------------
# Both boxes already answer on :80 with a 308 redirect to https, but the TLS
# handshake on :443 fails for EVERY hostname tried (aequitas.digital,
# www/node1/c1/rpc subdomains) — there is no valid certificate. So HTTPS is
# currently non-functional on both boxes and the only working entry point is
# :8080 directly. Installing this file (and pointing DNS at the box) is what
# fixes that.
#
# DEPLOY
# ------
#   sudo cp deploy/Caddyfile /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#   sudo caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#   sudo systemctl reload caddy
#
# Caddy obtains and renews the certificate automatically via Let's Encrypt
# (HTTP-01 over port 80). Port 80 must stay open and reachable — it already is.
# Do NOT put the ACME challenge behind the redirect; Caddy handles that itself.

aequitas.digital, www.aequitas.digital {
	# The node listens on plain HTTP on the loopback interface only. Caddy is
	# the only thing that should be publicly exposed on 80/443.
	#
	# X-Forwarded-For: Caddy sets this automatically, and the node's clientIP()
	# (x/humanity/keeper/register.go) only trusts the header when the TCP peer
	# is private/loopback — i.e. exactly this proxy. That combination is what
	# makes the per-IP rate limits count real client IPs instead of lumping
	# every request under 127.0.0.1. Do not add a trusted_proxies directive
	# that would forward this header from anywhere else.
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080

	# Compression is deliberately NOT enabled here. The node already spends
	# measurable CPU on gzip for /rpc responses (~3.5% in the 2026-07-27 CPU
	# profile); adding a second compression pass in front of it costs CPU on a
	# box whose throughput ceiling is already CPU-bound, for no gain.

	# Security headers are set per-route by the node itself (X-Frame-Options,
	# X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy, and a per-page CSP — see
	# handleUI/handleDapp/handleLanding in x/humanity/keeper/api.go).
	# Deliberately not duplicated here: a blanket header at the proxy would
	# silently override the per-page CSPs, which differ by page on purpose.

	log {
		output file /var/log/caddy/aequitas.log {
			roll_size 50MiB
			roll_keep 5
		}
	}
}

# NOTE — the libp2p P2P listener (port 4001) is NOT proxied and must not be.
# It speaks its own transport with its own encryption and peer-ID
# authentication, not HTTP. It needs to stay directly reachable on 4001/tcp,
# which is what the bootstrap multiaddrs in x/humanity/keeper/p2p.go point at.
# Verify with:  nc -vz <ip> 4001
